WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:02.202
- Let's say that I have a circle.

00:02.202 --> 00:05.667
My best attempt to draw a
reasonably perfect circle.

00:05.667 --> 00:09.700
So, there you go, not too
bad, it's a little bit

00:09.700 --> 00:11.366
of a hairy circle but you get the idea.

00:11.366 --> 00:16.067
So, this is a circle, this
is the center of the circle,

00:16.067 --> 00:19.267
and let's say that I have
an arc along this circle.

00:19.267 --> 00:21.900
So, I'll do the arc in green.

00:21.900 --> 00:26.700
So, I have an arc that
is part of the circle,

00:26.700 --> 00:30.800
and it subtends an
angle, so that's my arc.

00:30.800 --> 00:35.400
Right over there, and
it subtends an angle,

00:35.400 --> 00:38.068
and the angle that it subtends,
so what I mean subtends,

00:38.068 --> 00:40.033
you take each of the endpoints of the arc,

00:40.033 --> 00:42.733
go to the center of the circle,

00:42.733 --> 00:47.733
go to the center of the
circle just like this,

00:48.966 --> 00:53.966
and so it subtends angle
theta, right over here,

00:54.100 --> 00:57.033
so it subtends angle theta,
and let's say that we know

00:57.033 --> 01:02.033
that angle theta is equal to two radians.

01:02.367 --> 01:06.300
So my question to you is what fraction

01:06.300 --> 01:11.300
of the entire circumference
is this green arc?

01:11.333 --> 01:16.333
What fraction of the entire
circumference is this green arc?

01:17.267 --> 01:21.000
And like always, pause the
video, and give it a go.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.600
(laughs) All right, so let's
think through it a little bit.

01:24.600 --> 01:26.067
So, you might say well how do I know that,

01:26.067 --> 01:28.300
I don't know what the
radius of this thing is,

01:28.300 --> 01:32.166
I don't, how do I think through this?

01:32.166 --> 01:36.100
And we just have to remind
ourselves what radians mean,

01:36.100 --> 01:37.633
what radians mean.

01:37.633 --> 01:42.133
If an arc subtends the
angle of two radians,

01:42.133 --> 01:47.133
that means that the arc itself
is two "radiuseseses" long.

01:47.600 --> 01:49.066
(laughs) So, this right over here,

01:49.066 --> 01:51.766
let me make this a
little clearer, so this,

01:51.766 --> 01:56.533
if the radius is r, if this radius is,

01:56.533 --> 01:58.866
I already used that
color, if this radius...

01:58.866 --> 02:01.293
I have trouble switching
colors (laughs) all right.

02:01.293 --> 02:05.133
If this radius is length r,

02:05.133 --> 02:09.600
then the length, if this
angle is two radians,

02:09.600 --> 02:12.833
then the arc that
subtends it is going to be

02:12.833 --> 02:17.600
two radiuses long, so this
length right over here,

02:17.600 --> 02:20.333
is two radiuses.

02:20.333 --> 02:23.700
Now, what fraction of the
entire circumference is that?

02:23.700 --> 02:26.134
Well, the entire circumference, we know,

02:26.134 --> 02:27.400
we know this from basic geometry,

02:27.400 --> 02:31.233
the entire circumference
is two pi times the radius,

02:31.233 --> 02:34.800
or you can say it's two pi
radii, two pi "radiuseses",

02:34.800 --> 02:36.700
(laughs) two pi radii is
the correct way to say it.

02:36.700 --> 02:38.233
So, what fraction is it?

02:38.233 --> 02:41.800
It's two radii, it's two radii,

02:41.800 --> 02:46.800
over two pi radii, over two pi radii,

02:47.068 --> 02:51.666
twos cancel out, rs cancel
out, and so it is one "pith",

02:51.666 --> 02:53.172
(laughs) I guess you could say,

02:53.172 --> 02:57.172
it is one over pi of
the total circumference.
